WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.662
Hey, good morning, Ziad.

00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:03.670
Hope you got a good sleep in.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.163
It's Sunday, November twenty-second.

00:00:06.163 --> 00:00:10.733
A slow one on the calendar, with the weather turning on you later.

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:13.572
Here's one to think about while you move.

00:00:13.572 --> 00:00:21.190
What's the name for the trick where a loud sound hides the quiet ones near it, so an M.P.3 can just skip them?

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.574
Answer's at the end.

00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:25.137
Okay, weather actually matters today.

00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:35.454
It's mild this morning, low sixties, with maybe a stray shower, but the real storm moves in around two and gets heaviest from about five to midnight.

00:00:35.454 --> 00:00:47.226
Expect somewhere between half an inch and a couple of inches, puddles on the streets tonight, and gusts up near thirty or forty after dark, so tonight's not a bike night.

00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:48.196
Okay, the day.

00:00:48.196 --> 00:00:52.697
Your calendar has an editing block at ten-fifteen, about an hour.

00:00:52.697 --> 00:00:57.752
Then nothing until eight, when there's a plan on there for about an hour.

00:00:57.752 --> 00:01:05.508
That one lands right in the soggiest, windiest stretch, so if you're going, the train or a car is the easy call.

00:01:05.508 --> 00:01:06.477
Alright, news.

00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:07.516
Just two today.

00:01:07.516 --> 00:01:08.970
One from your corner.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:18.318
An artist in Britain named Stuart Semple put out a photo editor called Niva, pitched as a Photoshop alternative with no generative A.I.

00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:19.218
in it at all.

00:01:19.218 --> 00:01:23.789
It does photo editing, vector, and layout, in the browser for now.

00:01:23.789 --> 00:01:29.882
It's free for an hour a day, or a hundred bucks for a lifetime license, no subscription.

00:01:29.882 --> 00:01:35.768
Nearly thirty thousand people downloaded it in two days which kind of says something.

00:01:35.768 --> 00:01:37.569
And the money side of A.I.

00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:38.746
had a rough week.

00:01:38.746 --> 00:01:49.687
The Nasdaq hundred dropped about five and a half percent on Friday alone, and about ten percent for the week, apparently its worst run in more than six years.

00:01:49.687 --> 00:01:51.626
Chip stocks took most of it.

00:01:51.626 --> 00:02:02.429
Nvidia lost more than a fifth of its value over the week, and Taiwan's market fell hard enough that its regulator is banning short selling starting Tuesday.

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:05.752
Okay, here's the main thing for the coming week.

00:02:05.752 --> 00:02:11.569
Starting Saturday the twenty-eighth, Greenpoint is doing its fifth Shop Small crawl.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:18.286
It runs nine days, through Sunday, December sixth, and sixty-four independent shops are in on it.

00:02:18.286 --> 00:02:25.765
It's free to wander, and it's a nice excuse to knock out some holiday gifts at places that aren't a website.

00:02:25.765 --> 00:02:27.288
Oh, and a smaller one.

00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:33.867
The Bushwick Starr, the little theater on Eldert Street, does free readings of brand-new plays.

00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:37.675
There's one on Tuesday, December first, in the evening.

00:02:37.675 --> 00:02:39.406
You just need to R.S.V.P.

00:02:39.406 --> 00:02:44.946
It's actors with scripts in hand, and you hear a play before almost anyone else.

00:02:44.946 --> 00:02:47.301
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:47.301 --> 00:02:52.702
Once the rain starts and you're stuck inside, call someone instead of texting.

00:02:52.702 --> 00:02:55.056
An actual phone call, ten minutes.

00:02:55.056 --> 00:02:58.380
Pick someone you'd normally just send a meme to.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.189
People pick up more than you'd think on a rainy Sunday.

00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:04.751
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.244
Remember those plywood wall shelves?

00:03:07.244 --> 00:03:12.714
Here's why plywood stays flat, and what actually decides how much a shelf sags.

00:03:12.714 --> 00:03:13.891
Solid wood moves.

00:03:13.891 --> 00:03:18.669
It soaks up moisture from the air and swells, then dries and shrinks.

00:03:18.669 --> 00:03:21.509
Along the grain it barely changes at all.

00:03:21.509 --> 00:03:28.433
Across the grain it can move a few percent, which on a wide board is enough to cup, twist, or crack.

00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:29.818
Plywood cheats that.

00:03:29.818 --> 00:03:35.566
It's thin sheets of wood glued up with the grain turned ninety degrees every layer.

00:03:35.566 --> 00:03:42.283
Each layer wants to swell sideways, and the next layer, running the other way, just won't let it.

00:03:42.283 --> 00:03:48.792
And there's almost always an odd number of layers, so the outside faces match and pull evenly.

00:03:48.792 --> 00:03:50.869
That's why a sheet stays flat.

00:03:50.869 --> 00:03:52.046
Now the sag part.

00:03:52.046 --> 00:03:57.240
Two numbers matter way more than anything else, the span and the thickness.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.425
Sag goes up with the span to the fourth power.

00:04:00.425 --> 00:04:06.727
So if you double the distance between brackets, the shelf sags about sixteen times as much.

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:09.289
Thickness works the other way, cubed.

00:04:09.289 --> 00:04:13.236
Double the thickness and it sags about an eighth as much.

00:04:13.236 --> 00:04:15.313
Here's the catch with plywood.

00:04:15.313 --> 00:04:23.484
Since half its layers run crosswise, it's actually a little less stiff along its length than solid wood the same size.

00:04:23.484 --> 00:04:28.263
The classic fix is gluing a strip of solid wood along the front edge.

00:04:28.263 --> 00:04:34.979
It's like a tiny beam standing on its side, and it stiffens the shelf a lot for very little wood.

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:36.018
One last thing.

00:04:36.018 --> 00:04:39.965
Wood under a steady load keeps sagging slowly for months.

00:04:39.965 --> 00:04:41.350
That's called creep.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.297
Brackets a little closer together is the cheap insurance.

00:04:45.297 --> 00:04:50.906
So: calendar says editing at ten-fifteen and a plan at eight, right in the storm.

00:04:50.906 --> 00:04:53.053
Make one real phone call today.

00:04:53.053 --> 00:04:55.269
The answer: it's called masking.

00:04:55.269 --> 00:04:57.000
Stay dry out there, Ziad.
